---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { dict } from '../../utils/i18n';
const t = dict.en;
---

<BaseLayout title="Philosophy" description="Neonatox philosophy: learn by building, not using without understanding." lang="en">
  <section class="py-20 px-4">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-8">Not another distro</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-300 mb-8">
        Neonatox doesn't aim to be the best distro. It aims to be the most honest.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-8 text-slate-300">
        <div class="bg-slate-800/50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-cyan-300 mb-4">The black box</h2>
          <p>
            Modern distributions work incredibly well. Installing them takes minutes. Everything boots. Everything just works.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            But underneath that convenience lies a black box.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Thousands of lines of code running out of your sight. Systemd services spawning processes. Inherited configs you never touched. Precompiled packages whose origin and build remain hidden.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            There's nothing wrong with that.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            But if your goal is not just to use Linux, but to master it, you need to open the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-800/50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-cyan-300 mb-4">Build to understand</h2>
          <p>
            Linux From Scratch isn't just an installation guide. It's a transformative learning experience.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Compiling GCC teaches you how a compiler is born. Building Glibc reveals what actually holds up a Linux system. Configuring systemd forces you to understand how the system boots and organizes itself.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Neonatox is born from the same philosophy, but with a modern approach: keeping the deep learning of LFS while providing practical tools for real use — native package manager, clean separation between build and runtime dependencies, and documentation designed to teach, not just instruct.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-800/50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-cyan-300 mb-4">The package manager that teaches</h2>
          <p>
            <code>nhopkg</code> doesn't just install. Every package is a document.
            Open a <code>*.srcnho</code> and inside you see a <code>nhoid</code> with: build vs. runtime dependencies,
            build scripts with explicit variables, systemd integration.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            A package in Neonatox isn't just a downloaded binary or source. It's <strong>a project</strong> with history.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-800/50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-700">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-cyan-300 mb-4">Who is this for?</h2>
          <p>
            If you install Ubuntu, everything works and you never wonder why, you probably don't need Neonatox.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            But if you've ever wondered:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-slate-300 space-y-2 mt-4 pl-4">
            <li>What actually happens when you run <code>systemctl start</code>?</li>
            <li>Why does this package drag 47 dependencies?</li>
            <li>How do you compile GCC from scratch?</li>
            <li>What does the system actually do during boot?</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-cyan-400 font-medium">
            Then you probably do. Welcome.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 flex gap-4">
        <a href="/en/arquitectura" class="bg-cyan-500 hover:bg-cyan-400 text-slate-900 font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          See the architecture
        </a>
        <a href="/en/ruta-constructor" class="border border-cyan-500 text-cyan-400 hover:bg-cyan-500/10 font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Start the path
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>